## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET Core+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed** — no equivalent exists
3. The cooperative cancellation model (`CancellationToken`) replaces forced thread termination

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Will throw if the task doesn't complete in time
            task.Wait(timeout);
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerException is not TimeoutException)
        {
            throw; // Preserve original error behavior
        }

        if (!task.IsCompleted)
        {
            cts.Cancel();
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        // Unwraps AggregateException to match original single-exception behavior,
        // or rethrows AggregateException if multiple inner exceptions exist
        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
}
```

### If the work itself needs to be cancellable

The above only abandons the task on timeout — it **does not forcefully kill** the work (nothing in .NET 8 can). For true cooperative cancellation, accept a token:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
                   .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Callers then check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` periodically.

### Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Fire-and-forget leak** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, an abandoned `Task.Run` keeps running until it finishes or the process exits. Long-running CPU work won't stop without checking the token. |
| **Deadlocks** | `.Wait()` / `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` on the calling thread can deadlock if `work` posts back to a synchronization context (e.g., UI thread). Use `Task.Run` to avoid this. |
| **Exception wrapping** | `.Wait()` wraps in `AggregateException`; `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` unwraps to the first inner exception. The migrated code uses `GetResult()` to match the original single-exception throw. |
| **`default` return value gone** | The original code's `T result = default` was a silent-failure path if the thread was aborted. That path no longer exists — the migrated code always throws on timeout. This is *safer*. |
| **Process.Kill as last resort** | If you truly need forced termination of untrusted code, the only .NET 8 option is running it in a **separate process** and calling `Process.Kill()`. |
